msgid "Mail"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:76
msgid "Settings"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:89
#: includes/admin.php:112
msgid "You are not allowed to do that."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:97
msgid "That is not an address."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:151
msgid "The test message was accepted by the mail server."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:151
msgid "The test message did not go out."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:155
msgid "Accepted is not the same as delivered. Look in the inbox, and then in the spam folder — which of the two it landed in is what the records below decide."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:158
msgid "The exchange with the mail server:"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:164
msgid "Whether this will land in the inbox"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:169
msgid "Sending through"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:172
msgid "a mail server —"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:174
msgid "the web server’s own mail() — unauthenticated, and the usual reason mail is refused or filed as spam"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:179
msgid "Sending as"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:186
msgid "cannot be checked — this server does not allow DNS lookups"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:190
msgid "It ends in -all, which asks receiving servers to refuse anything sent from elsewhere rather than merely doubt it. That is the right setting, and it means the mail server used here has to be one the record allows."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:193
msgid "missing — anybody may send as this domain, and receiving servers know it"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:205
msgid "missing"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:207
msgid "Worth adding, and harmless to start with. A first record asks for reports without changing how anything is delivered:"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:216
msgid "set up at the mail provider, under a name it chooses — there is no way to look for it from here. Ask the provider whether it signs outgoing mail."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:219
msgid "Checking for certain"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:221
msgid "Send a test to a Gmail address, open it there and choose “Show original”. The first lines give the receiving end’s own verdict — spf: PASS, dkim: PASS, dmarc: PASS — and that is the only answer that counts. Nothing checked from this side can tell you, because SPF is judged on the machine that delivers the message, not on the one this site hands it to."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: a domain name.
#: includes/admin.php:231
#, php-format
msgid "These records belong to the domain %s and live with whoever holds it, not in WordPress. Sending through a mail server settles who is sending; these decide whether the receiving end believes it. Without them a perfectly configured site still lands in the spam folder."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:240
msgid "The mail server"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:244
msgid "Use it"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:246
msgid "Send through SMTP instead of the web server"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:250
msgid "Server and port"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:255
msgid "STARTTLS (usually port 587)"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:256
msgid "TLS from the start (usually port 465)"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:257
msgid "No encryption"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:259
msgid "The host gives these out with the mailbox. Without encryption the password crosses the network in the clear, so only choose that for a server on the same machine."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:263
msgid "Signing in"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:265
msgid "The server asks for a user name and password"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:267
msgid "user name"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:270
msgid "password"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:270
msgid "leave empty to keep the one saved"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:274
msgid "The password is set as a constant in a file rather than in the database, which is where it is best kept. Anything typed here is ignored."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:276
msgid "Forget the saved password"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:278
msgid "It is kept in the database, encrypted with a key made from this site’s salts — which live in wp-config.php and not in the database. That protects a database dump, which is how these usually get out; it protects nothing against somebody already on the server."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:279
msgid "Better still is a constant in a file the database knows nothing about — wp-config.php, or a must-use plugin: define( 'WOW_MAIL_PASSWORD', '…' );"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:282
msgid "Better than typing it here is a constant in a file, which keeps it out of the database and out of every backup of it — in wp-config.php, or in a must-use plugin: define( 'WOW_MAIL_PASSWORD', '…' );"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:288
msgid "Who the mail is from"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:292
msgid "Address and name"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:296
msgid "Use it for everything the site sends"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:298
msgid "It has to be a mailbox that exists, and at the same domain the mail server signs us in as. WordPress otherwise sends as wordpress@ the site’s domain, which is usually nothing at all — and a From address that bounces is read by a spam filter as exactly what it looks like."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:299
msgid "A message sent through one provider’s server claiming to come from another’s domain has the shape of a forgery and is treated as one, however well everything else is set up."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:304
msgid "Record"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:306
msgid "Keep a short record of what was sent"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:307
msgid "Who it was for and what it was about, for a fortnight. Never the message itself."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:315
msgid "Send a test"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:321
msgid "Send"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin.php:322
msgid "If it fails, the whole exchange with the mail server is shown — which greeting failed, which password was refused, which certificate was not trusted. That is nearly always where the answer is."
msgstr ""

